  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request with:\n- **Request details:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`, `agent`\n- **Outcome:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as a string such as \"503\") and `bytes`\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `geo.src`/`geo.dest`/`geo.coordinates`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `memory`, `hour_of_day`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-e31b0f54 Web logs overview\"** (id `6ec6e4c3-df7a-4c77-a6d4-cc57d6ed7c26`, default time range is the last 7 days). It has six panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct `clientip`)\n- Average response size in bytes\n- 5xx error rate as a percentage, built with an ES|QL query\n- Requests over time as stacked bars, split by response code\n- Top 10 requested URLs\n\nKibana accepted all six panels, but I didn't open the dashboard in the browser. The error-rate panel is the one most worth a quick look, because I didn't run its query by itself.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-e31b0f54 5xx spike\"** (id `69704a73-874b-404c-9351-d0418f9109d2`) is an Index threshold rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute, counts requests whose response code starts with 5 over the last 5 minutes, and fires when that count is above 10.\n- **Status:** enabled, and its first run succeeded with no alerts.\n- **Filter check:** the 5xx filter matched 44 requests over the past 7 days, so it does pick up the right documents.\n- **No notifications:** the rule has no actions attached, so it will raise alerts in Kibana but won't email or message anyone. Add a connector if you want notifications.",
